{
    /* SELF-CONTAINED ON PURPOSE — do not reintroduce `extends: "../../tsconfig.json"`.

       Angular's migration schematics (`ng generate @angular/core:signal-input-migration`,
       `control-flow-migration`, and the rest of the `signals` family) run against a virtual
       filesystem rooted at the Angular workspace, which for this project is `packages/angulux`.
       `@angular/compiler-cli`'s `readAngularCompilerOptions` follows `extends` recursively and
       has NO cycle guard, so an `extends` that escapes the workspace root gets clamped back to
       the root — that is, back to this file — and the function calls itself until the stack
       gives out:

           Preparing analysis for: tsconfig.json...
           Tsconfig could not be parsed or is invalid:
           RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded
             at readAngularCompilerOptions (@angular/compiler-cli/bundles/chunk-JTB6ADF4.js:471)

       It fails before writing anything, so nothing was ever corrupted by it — it simply meant
       that none of the official modernization tooling could be run on the library, and nobody
       knew, because nobody had tried. Removing the `extends` was verified to fix it: the same
       command then migrates `src/divider` cleanly, 4/4 inputs, updating the spec alongside the
       component.

       `apps/verify` and `apps/showcase` were already self-contained; this file was the only
       Angular workspace config in the repo reaching outside itself. The duplication below is
       the price, and it is the cheaper side of the trade: the root `tsconfig.json` is the
       editor and tooling config for the whole monorepo, and nothing forces the library — the
       one thing here that is published — to inherit from it.

       The values below were lifted from the resolved output of the previous chain, not
       retyped from memory: `readConfiguration()` reports the same 24 options and the same
       2901 root files before and after. `paths` is the one entry that had to change SHAPE,
       because TypeScript resolves path mappings relative to the config that declares them —
       `./packages/angulux/src/*` from the root is `./src/*` from here, and both land on the
       same directory. */
    "compilerOptions": {
        "target": "ES2022",
        "lib": ["dom", "dom.iterable", "ES2022"],
        "experimentalDecorators": true,
        "module": "ES2022",
        "moduleResolution": "bundler",
        "paths": {
            "@anguless/angulux/types/*": ["./src/types/*/public_api"],
            "@anguless/angulux/*": ["./src/*/public_api"]
        },
        "resolveJsonModule": true,
        "esModuleInterop": true,
        "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
        "strict": false,
        "strictNullChecks": true,
        "skipLibCheck": true
    },
    /* Inherited verbatim from the root config, which is why the globs climb out of this
       directory: they used to be resolved against the root, and the program they select is
       part of what "no behaviour change" means here. Narrowing them is a separate decision
       with its own evidence, not a side effect of unblocking the schematics. */
    "include": ["../../**/src/**/*", "../../**/dist/**/*", "../../**/types/**/*"],
    "exclude": ["node_modules", "attic", "dist"],
    "angularCompilerOptions": {
        "disableTypeScriptVersionCheck": true,
        "fullTemplateTypeCheck": false,
        "annotateForClosureCompiler": false,
        "strictTemplates": false,
        "strictMetadataEmit": true,
        "skipTemplateCodegen": true,
        "enableI18nLegacyMessageIdFormat": false,
        "strictInjectionParameters": true,
        "strictInputAccessModifiers": true,
        "flatModuleOutFile": "primeng.js",
        "flatModuleId": "primeng",
        "compilationMode": "partial"
    }
}
